# OPEN positive. A formal handed on may hold a callable built where the call
# is made, and one site's merge does not change what another site gives.
# take calls its formal; mid hands its own formal to take. The root gives
# mid g1, which reads other: 10. passed gives mid a merge of five with its
# y bound: 5. g1 and the merge's node are formed differently, and the node a
# merge builds is no occurrence of the unit: this implementation does not
# tell its class where the formal is called, and the call in take is
# refused where it stands, as a limit and no rule. Entry 7.
int: other 9

fn: five (int: y) int
    return: y

fn: g1 () int
    return: other + 1
end: g1

fn: take (g1: op) int
    return: op()
end: take

fn: mid (g1: p) int
    return: take(p)
end: mid

fn: passed () int
    return: mid: merge(y: 5; five)
end: passed

int: a mid(g1)
int: d passed()
int: ok 7
if: a != 10 || d != 5
    ok: 81
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: ok; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
